<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bibracte</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bibracte was the chief hill-fort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, a large fortified Gallic town on Mont Beuvray in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. In 58 BCE Caesar defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> nearby, and in 52 BCE a Gallic assembly there confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> as leader of the revolt. The town was abandoned in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Augustodunum</a>, modern Autun, around the turn of the era, and is now an excavated archaeological site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bibracte.jpg" alt="Bibracte" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iijjccoo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bibracte333.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bibracte was one of the most important stone hillforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. It stood on Mont Beuvray, which straddles the French départements of Nièvre and Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, about 25 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>. The material culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> matched the Late Iron Age La Tène culture. Bibracte is mentioned only twice in Roman sources, both in Caesar's Commentaries on the Gallic War, in 58 and 52 BCE. The origin of its name is poorly understood: it may come from a Celtic word for beaver, or from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> biffractrus, meaning twice fortified, though excavation shows that two walls did not stand at once.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dating shows that the oppidum was founded at the end of the 3rd century BCE, within an outer rampart enclosing 200 hectares. An inner rampart of the Murus Gallicus type, about 5 kilometres long, was built later and enclosed 135 hectares, so the city shrank. Before the Roman conquest it had more than thirty thousand inhabitants. Caesar describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> as allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, called blood brothers, since at least the 2nd century BCE, and as rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> and Sequani for hegemony over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> helped them defeat an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> army in that century and defended them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a>, whom Caesar's armies defeated in 58 BCE, 26 kilometres from the fort. In 52 BCE Vercingetorix was proclaimed head of the Gaulish coalition at Bibracte. A few decades after the conquest the site was abandoned in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>, called Augustodunum, and then lay undisturbed until modern archaeology.</p><p>Excavation has found a district of specialised metalworkers near the Rebout gate: blacksmiths, bronzeworkers and enamellers, probably also goldsmiths and minters. Houses were mostly of wood and earth, with stone kept for the ramparts. A Roman-style basilica with three naves, dated between 50 and 40 BCE and between 35 and 25 BCE, is the oldest known example of Roman monumental stone architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and it shows that the Romanization of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> was faster than once thought.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Jacques-Gabriel Bulliot identified Bibracte in 1867 and excavated from 1867 to 1895, with funds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>. His nephew Joseph Déchelette continued from 1897 to 1907. Work resumed in 1984, and the site was declared of national interest in 1985. Teams from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, Sheffield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> have worked there. The site is now an archaeological park in a protected forest, with a Museum of Celtic Civilization that opened in 1996 and a European research centre at Glux-en-Glenne. It received the Great Site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> designation on 12 December 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bibracte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 2nd century BCE large fortified towns, which Caesar called oppida, grew up from France to Bohemia, among them Manching in Bavaria and <b>Bibracte</b>, the hill town of the Aedui on Mont Beuvray.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Peoples were divided into tribes, usually ruled by kings, and allied tribes could choose a war leader, as an assembly of Gauls at <b>Bibracte</b> chose Vercingetorix against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Large fortified towns, the oppida, such as <b>Bibracte</b>, Avaricum and Gergovia, were centres of population, and Roman merchants came to trade.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aedui held the land between the Loire and the Saône, in modern Burgundy, with their great oppidum at <b>Bibracte</b> on Mont Beuvray.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of the Helvetii near <b>Bibracte</b>, the Aedui asked Caesar to let the Boii settle in their land because of their courage.</blockquote><a href="/state/boii-of-gaul">Boii of Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Following a plan of the noble Orgetorix, who died before it began, they burned their towns and set off west in 58 BCE, but Caesar defeated them near <b>Bibracte</b> and sent the survivors home.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li></ul></section>
